Performance does not degrade smoothly as $h$ decreases: a credit assignment cliff between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ separates conditions that learn from those that collapse. Below $h{=}10$, the context window is too narrow for the policy to represent multi-step strategies, and sign misattribution compounds across the many short windows. Above $h{=}20$, sufficient context enables partial recovery. Only episode-level training, which preserves the full trajectory, achieves substantial generalization.

